{
 "id": "retarget",
 "kicker": "Retargeting",
 "title": "A human body on G1 proportions",
 "tagline": "Rebuilding the person on the G1's own bone lengths keeps their joint angles (2.4–10× smaller angle error) and plants the feet: foot sliding drops from 29.1 to 0.4 cm/s, averaged over 7 clips.",
 "story": {
  "shown": "The same squat-jump video, retargeted two ways. Uniform scaling shrinks the whole person by leg length; the G1's hips are still about 60% wider than the scaled human's, so the inverse kinematics chases targets no G1 pose can reach: feet slide and elbows straighten.",
  "learned": "Proportional retargeting walks the skeleton from the hips and gives every bone the human's direction but the G1's length, then pins each planted foot to the floor. Every joint angle is the human's, and the hands and feet land where the G1 can actually reach.",
  "how": "Measured on all 7 recorded clips against the human's own joint angles and foot contacts. The angle columns compare robot and human flexion at the knee, hip, elbow and shoulder. The bars show the 7-clip mean; the video is squat_jump."
 },
